1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:04,410
There is a strange problem with finding
planets outside our Solar System.

2
00:00:04,900 --> 00:00:08,550
They are everywhere. But
we can barely see them.

3
00:00:09,290 --> 00:00:10,810
Imagine pointing the most powerful

4
00:00:11,040 --> 00:00:13,790
telescope ever built
at another star system.

5
00:00:14,510 --> 00:00:17,630
You might expect to see
something like this: A star.

6
00:00:18,030 --> 00:00:20,250
And next to it, a planet. Simple.

7
00:00:20,480 --> 00:00:23,330
Except that almost never works.

8
00:00:24,290 --> 00:00:27,870
A planet is incredibly faint
compared with the star it orbits.

9
00:00:28,790 --> 00:00:31,870
And from dozens or hundreds
of light-years away, the star

10
00:00:32,100 --> 00:00:35,230
and the planet appear almost
like a single point of light.

11
00:00:36,220 --> 00:00:39,820
So how do astronomers find
planets they usually cannot see?

12
00:00:40,420 --> 00:00:42,480
The answer is surprisingly clever.

13
00:00:43,610 --> 00:00:45,830
They don't necessarily
look for the planet.

14
00:00:46,450 --> 00:00:49,410
They look for what the planet
does to everything around it.

15
00:00:49,970 --> 00:00:52,170
And there are several ways to do that.

16
00:00:53,350 --> 00:00:57,290
Let's start with the most
obvious one. Direct imaging.

17
00:00:57,690 --> 00:01:00,390
If we can somehow block
the overwhelming light of a

18
00:01:00,620 --> 00:01:03,930
star, a sufficiently bright
planet can appear next to it.

19
00:01:04,270 --> 00:01:06,230
This is not science fiction.

20
00:01:07,010 --> 00:01:10,450
Astronomers have directly
imaged some exoplanets.

21
00:01:11,320 --> 00:01:12,700
But there is a catch.

22
00:01:13,240 --> 00:01:18,140
The planets we can image directly tend to
be large, young, and still relatively hot.

23
00:01:19,180 --> 00:01:22,380
A young planet can glow strongly
in infrared light because

24
00:01:22,610 --> 00:01:25,920
it is still radiating heat
left over from its formation.

25
00:01:26,340 --> 00:01:30,920
Even then, separating the planet
from its star is extremely difficult.

26
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:35,440
A telescope may need a special
instrument called a coronagraph.

27
00:01:36,080 --> 00:01:39,540
A coronagraph blocks or
suppresses the light from the star

28
00:01:39,620 --> 00:01:42,920
while allowing light from nearby
objects to reach the detector.

29
00:01:44,150 --> 00:01:47,630
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

30
00:01:48,250 --> 00:01:49,870
It really helps the channel.

31
00:01:50,640 --> 00:01:52,720
And even after all
that, we are usually not

32
00:01:52,950 --> 00:01:55,200
looking at a detailed picture of a planet.

33
00:01:55,720 --> 00:01:58,100
We are looking at a tiny point of light.

34
00:01:58,640 --> 00:02:02,420
But that tiny point can contain an
astonishing amount of information.

35
00:02:03,220 --> 00:02:06,120
If we split its light into a
spectrum, we can look for the

36
00:02:06,350 --> 00:02:09,740
chemical fingerprints of molecules
in the planet's atmosphere.

37
00:02:10,520 --> 00:02:15,160
So direct imaging can potentially tell
us not just: "There is something there."

38
00:02:15,600 --> 00:02:19,560
But: "There is a planet there — and this
is what its atmosphere may contain."

39
00:02:21,120 --> 00:02:23,420
Still, direct imaging is difficult.

40
00:02:24,480 --> 00:02:26,520
Fortunately, there is a much easier trick.

41
00:02:27,020 --> 00:02:29,300
We can wait for the
planet to get in the way.

42
00:02:31,630 --> 00:02:34,310
Imagine looking at a star from far away.

43
00:02:34,610 --> 00:02:38,590
Now imagine a planet crossing
directly between the star and us.

44
00:02:39,470 --> 00:02:42,350
The planet blocks a tiny
fraction of the star's light.

45
00:02:43,070 --> 00:02:45,010
The star gets slightly dimmer.

46
00:02:45,530 --> 00:02:48,810
And then, when the planet moves
away, the star becomes bright again.

47
00:02:49,410 --> 00:02:50,610
That's a transit.

48
00:02:51,440 --> 00:02:53,640
The change can be incredibly small.

49
00:02:54,440 --> 00:02:57,980
For an Earth-sized planet crossing
a Sun-like star, the drop in

50
00:02:58,210 --> 00:03:02,417
brightness is only about zero
point zero zero eight per cent.

51
00:03:02,716 --> 00:03:05,496
That's about one part in twelve thousand.

52
00:03:05,816 --> 00:03:08,256
A tiny change in a very bright signal.

53
00:03:08,516 --> 00:03:12,316
But modern telescopes can measure
tiny changes in brightness.

54
00:03:13,116 --> 00:03:15,096
And if the same dip
happens again and again at

55
00:03:15,326 --> 00:03:20,376
regular intervals, we have something
very interesting. A planet candidate.

56
00:03:21,296 --> 00:03:25,576
Other causes, such as an eclipsing pair
of stars, have to be ruled out first.

57
00:03:26,656 --> 00:03:29,936
The time between the dips tells
us the planet's orbital period.

58
00:03:30,696 --> 00:03:32,496
The depth of the dip
tells us approximately

59
00:03:32,726 --> 00:03:35,576
how large the planet is
compared with its star.

60
00:03:36,156 --> 00:03:39,216
And the shape and duration of
the transit can reveal additional

61
00:03:39,446 --> 00:03:43,036
information about the orbit
and the geometry of the system.

62
00:03:44,136 --> 00:03:45,796
This is one of the reasons missions such

63
00:03:46,026 --> 00:03:48,616
as Kepler and TESS have
been so successful.

64
00:03:49,016 --> 00:03:52,456
They don't need to photograph
thousands of planets individually.

65
00:03:53,076 --> 00:03:55,236
They simply watch
enormous numbers of stars

66
00:03:55,466 --> 00:03:58,476
and ask: "Did any of them
get slightly darker?"

67
00:03:59,406 --> 00:04:01,586
But there is an important limitation.

68
00:04:02,406 --> 00:04:05,866
A transit only happens if the
planet's orbit happens to be aligned

69
00:04:05,946 --> 00:04:09,006
so that the planet crosses the
star from our point of view.

70
00:04:09,546 --> 00:04:12,426
A planet can be there
and never transit at all.

71
00:04:13,696 --> 00:04:15,436
So what if we could
detect a planet without

72
00:04:15,666 --> 00:04:19,536
waiting for it to pass in
front of its star? We can.

73
00:04:20,016 --> 00:04:22,476
And this next method
is particularly clever.

74
00:04:24,886 --> 00:04:28,546
It is tempting to imagine a
planet orbiting a stationary star.

75
00:04:28,906 --> 00:04:31,086
But that's not quite what happens.

76
00:04:31,946 --> 00:04:35,526
A planet and its star both orbit
their common centre of mass.

77
00:04:36,026 --> 00:04:38,626
Usually the star is much more massive, so

78
00:04:38,856 --> 00:04:41,426
the centre of mass is
very close to the star.

79
00:04:41,896 --> 00:04:43,896
But the star still moves. Tiny amounts.

80
00:04:44,126 --> 00:04:48,096
And that movement can be detected.
How? With a spectrograph.

81
00:04:49,112 --> 00:04:52,572
A spectrograph spreads starlight
into its component wavelengths.

82
00:04:53,532 --> 00:04:56,272
Stellar spectra contain dark
absorption lines produced

83
00:04:56,502 --> 00:04:59,792
by atoms and molecules
in stellar atmospheres.

84
00:05:00,432 --> 00:05:02,352
If the star moves
slightly towards us, those

85
00:05:02,582 --> 00:05:05,072
spectral lines shift
towards shorter wavelengths.

86
00:05:05,852 --> 00:05:09,112
If it moves away from us, they
shift towards longer wavelengths.

87
00:05:10,072 --> 00:05:11,812
This is the Doppler effect.

88
00:05:12,502 --> 00:05:15,542
So instead of seeing the planet,
we watch the star's spectrum.

89
00:05:16,102 --> 00:05:18,722
If the spectral lines
repeatedly move back and

90
00:05:18,952 --> 00:05:21,742
forth, something may be
pulling the star around.

91
00:05:22,442 --> 00:05:24,982
And one possible explanation is a planet.

92
00:05:26,002 --> 00:05:29,562
This technique is usually called
the radial velocity method.

93
00:05:30,202 --> 00:05:34,942
It can tell us about the planet's orbital
period and the size of the star's motion.

94
00:05:35,482 --> 00:05:38,142
And from that, with an
estimate of the star's mass,

95
00:05:38,582 --> 00:05:41,662
astronomers can estimate the
planet's mass — more precisely,

96
00:05:41,982 --> 00:05:45,402
its minimum mass unless the
orbital inclination is known.

97
00:05:46,982 --> 00:05:50,782
Now something really interesting
happens when we combine the two methods.

98
00:05:51,562 --> 00:05:54,342
Suppose a planet passes
in front of its star.

99
00:05:55,002 --> 00:05:58,482
The transit tells us the planet's
size relative to its star.

100
00:05:59,022 --> 00:06:02,682
If we know the star's size, we can
work out the planet's size too.

101
00:06:03,582 --> 00:06:08,082
The radial velocity method tells us how
strongly the planet pulls on its star.

102
00:06:08,882 --> 00:06:11,222
That gives us information about its mass.

103
00:06:11,862 --> 00:06:15,702
And if we know both mass and
size, we can calculate density.

104
00:06:16,862 --> 00:06:20,122
Suddenly, the planet stops
being just a mysterious dot.

105
00:06:20,462 --> 00:06:23,842
We can start asking what it
might actually be made of.

106
00:06:24,242 --> 00:06:27,462
A planet with a relatively
high density could be rocky.

107
00:06:28,492 --> 00:06:31,772
A much lower density could indicate
a large atmosphere surrounding

108
00:06:32,002 --> 00:06:36,332
a smaller solid core, or a
planet rich in water or gas.

109
00:06:37,272 --> 00:06:41,152
But density alone cannot tell us
exactly what a planet is made of.

110
00:06:41,942 --> 00:06:45,902
And this is one of the most powerful
ideas in exoplanet astronomy:

111
00:06:46,342 --> 00:06:49,622
Sometimes different methods
don't just find the same planet.

112
00:06:49,822 --> 00:06:52,322
They answer different parts of the puzzle.

113
00:06:55,042 --> 00:06:57,322
But there is an even stranger method.

114
00:06:57,922 --> 00:07:00,662
One where we don't need the
planet to cross its star.

115
00:07:00,862 --> 00:07:03,642
And we don't even need
to watch the star wobble.

116
00:07:04,762 --> 00:07:07,522
Imagine a distant star
behind another star.

117
00:07:08,282 --> 00:07:10,722
As the foreground star
passes in front of it, its

118
00:07:10,952 --> 00:07:13,602
gravity bends the light
from the background star.

119
00:07:14,662 --> 00:07:17,862
The foreground star acts
like a gravitational lens.

120
00:07:18,602 --> 00:07:21,322
The background star
temporarily appears brighter.

121
00:07:22,402 --> 00:07:25,222
Now add a planet orbiting
the foreground star.

122
00:07:25,962 --> 00:07:29,042
The planet's gravity can produce
an additional distortion.

123
00:07:29,722 --> 00:07:31,722
A brief anomaly in the brightening curve.

124
00:07:31,952 --> 00:07:34,342
That anomaly can reveal the planet.

125
00:07:35,322 --> 00:07:38,082
This is called gravitational microlensing.

126
00:07:38,802 --> 00:07:41,062
And it is particularly
useful for finding planets

127
00:07:41,292 --> 00:07:43,962
that can be difficult to
detect by other methods.

128
00:07:45,022 --> 00:07:47,782
But microlensing has a
strange disadvantage.

129
00:07:48,502 --> 00:07:50,622
The event may happen only once.

130
00:07:51,422 --> 00:07:54,142
After the stars move apart,
the alignment is gone.

131
00:07:54,662 --> 00:07:57,202
So unlike a transit, where
we can watch the same planet

132
00:07:57,432 --> 00:08:00,522
pass in front of its star
again and again, a microlensing

133
00:08:00,602 --> 00:08:03,822
event can be more like catching
a rare cosmic coincidence.

134
00:08:04,822 --> 00:08:06,522
One moment, something happens.

135
00:08:06,752 --> 00:08:08,902
Then it's gone.

136
00:08:11,502 --> 00:08:13,922
Finding a planet is only the beginning.

137
00:08:14,742 --> 00:08:17,542
Astronomers can sometimes
study its atmosphere by looking

138
00:08:17,772 --> 00:08:20,522
at starlight that passes
through it during a transit.

139
00:08:21,722 --> 00:08:24,222
Imagine the planet moving across the star.

140
00:08:24,782 --> 00:08:27,602
Most of the starlight
travels directly to us.

141
00:08:28,202 --> 00:08:31,782
But a tiny fraction passes through
the planet's atmosphere first.

142
00:08:32,762 --> 00:08:36,622
Different atoms and molecules absorb
different wavelengths of light.

143
00:08:37,402 --> 00:08:40,262
So when astronomers compare the
star's spectrum during a transit

144
00:08:40,492 --> 00:08:44,602
with its spectrum outside the
transit, they may see tiny changes.

145
00:08:45,692 --> 00:08:50,192
Those changes can reveal the fingerprints
of atoms and molecules in the atmosphere.

146
00:08:51,112 --> 00:08:56,392
Water vapour. Carbon
dioxide. Methane. Sodium.

147
00:08:57,032 --> 00:09:00,152
And other substances, depending on
the planet and the observations.

148
00:09:01,602 --> 00:09:03,922
The signals can be incredibly faint.

149
00:09:04,662 --> 00:09:06,862
But modern instruments can measure them.

150
00:09:06,942 --> 00:09:09,802
So a planet that is completely
invisible in an ordinary

151
00:09:10,032 --> 00:09:13,322
image can leave a fingerprint
in the light of its star.

152
00:09:15,852 --> 00:09:18,452
And that brings us back
to the original question.

153
00:09:19,212 --> 00:09:21,192
How do we find exoplanets?

154
00:09:21,692 --> 00:09:23,652
Sometimes we actually image them.

155
00:09:23,912 --> 00:09:26,652
But much more often, we
detect their effects.

156
00:09:27,652 --> 00:09:30,292
A planet can make its
star slightly dimmer.

157
00:09:30,732 --> 00:09:32,432
It can make its star move.

158
00:09:32,972 --> 00:09:35,952
It can distort the light from
a distant background star.

159
00:09:36,672 --> 00:09:39,552
Its atmosphere can alter
the spectrum of starlight.

160
00:09:40,172 --> 00:09:42,452
In other words, astronomers
have developed several

161
00:09:42,682 --> 00:09:45,752
ways of detecting something
without directly seeing it.

162
00:09:45,832 --> 00:09:49,612
And that is perhaps the
most fascinating part.

163
00:09:50,102 --> 00:09:52,802
When you hear that astronomers
have discovered an exoplanet

164
00:09:53,032 --> 00:09:56,322
hundreds of light-years away, it
does not necessarily mean that

165
00:09:56,552 --> 00:10:00,122
a telescope took a photograph
of a little blue or green world.

166
00:10:00,662 --> 00:10:03,562
Often, the evidence is much more subtle.

167
00:10:04,452 --> 00:10:05,472
A tiny dip in brightness.

168
00:10:05,702 --> 00:10:09,652
A microscopic shift in a spectral line.

169
00:10:09,812 --> 00:10:11,632
A brief gravitational distortion.

170
00:10:11,862 --> 00:10:15,092
A few missing wavelengths in a spectrum.

171
00:10:15,662 --> 00:10:21,462
And from these tiny signals, astronomers
reconstruct an entire world. Its orbit.

172
00:10:21,782 --> 00:10:24,382
Its size. Its mass. Its density.

173
00:10:24,502 --> 00:10:27,662
And sometimes even clues
about its atmosphere.

174
00:10:28,712 --> 00:10:32,092
All from light that has travelled
across the galaxy to reach us.

175
00:10:32,632 --> 00:10:35,812
The planet itself may be
almost impossible to see.

176
00:10:36,152 --> 00:10:39,692
But it cannot completely hide
the consequences of being there.

177
00:10:39,852 --> 00:10:42,462
And that's how we find
worlds we cannot see.
